  "youtube": {
    "title": "America almost had a 14th state called Franklin",
    "description": "In 1784, North Carolina offered its lands west of the Appalachians to Congress. Settlers in what is now East Tennessee met at Jonesborough, declared their own state and named it Franklin, after Benjamin Franklin, hoping to become the 14th state. North Carolina repealed the cession and reclaimed the land, and Franklin's bid for statehood fell short of the nine states needed under the Articles of Confederation. Its governor, John Sevier, was arrested for treason in 1788 but never tried; in 1796 he became the first governor of Tennessee, the 16th state.\n\nSources: Wikipedia, \"State of Franklin\"; Tennessee Encyclopedia (Tennessee Historical Society), \"State of Franklin\" and \"John Sevier\"; NCpedia, \"Franklin, State of\"; North Carolina History Project, \"State of Franklin\"; Jackson Kuhl, \"The First Free State Project\", Reason (2004); Tennessee Department of State, \"State Facts\".\nImages: public domain. \"Daniel Boone Escorting Settlers through the Cumberland Gap\" (George Caleb Bingham, 1851-52, used as illustration), portraits of Benjamin Franklin (Joseph-Siffred Duplessis, National Portrait Gallery) and John Sevier (Charles Willson Peale, c. 1792), and John Reid's 1795 map of the Tennessee government (Library of Congress), via Wikimedia Commons. Maps: Natural Earth (public domain); Franklin's area is approximate.\n\n#history #shorts #tennessee",
